INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Ronin, Harmony Horizon, and laundering analysis", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-002; SRC-003", "text": "Tool-returned evidence: Elliptic reports that the March 2022 Ronin Network bridge theft involved 173,600 ETH and 25.5 million USDC, valued at $540 million at theft; OFAC later sanctioned the thief's Ethereum address and identified its owner as Lazarus Group, a North Korean state hacking group (SRC-003). Ronin's postmortem said the attacker obtained five of nine validator private keys and that evidence pointed to social engineering. Elliptic says stolen USDC was swapped for ETH through DEXs, then $16.7m ETH was sent through three centralized exchanges; after public cooperation announcements, laundering shifted to Tornado Cash; $80.3m ETH had gone through Tornado Cash and $9.7m remained in intermediary wallets, with $433m in the original wallet as of April 14, 2022 (SRC-003). Chainalysis reports access to five of nine Ronin validator keys, withdrawals of 173,600 ETH and 25.5m USDC, more than 12,000 addresses used, and a typical North Korea-linked DeFi laundering sequence: intermediary wallets, Tornado Cash mixing, ETH-to-BTC swap, Bitcoin mixing, then crypto-to-fiat cashout. It also reports chain hopping through Ethereum, BNB, and BitTorrent chains and more than $30m seized, approximately 10% of the stolen funds (SRC-002). Elliptic reports the FBI confirmed Lazarus Group was behind the June 24, 2022 Harmony Horizon Bridge hack, which stole $99.7m. Elliptic says laundering was programmatically structured through Tornado Cash and mirrored Ronin laundering; it estimates more than $555m from these hacks passed through Tornado Cash, including over $468m from Ronin and $96m from Harmony. After Tornado Cash sanctions, funds were structured into Railgun in January 2023; around 70% of funds sent through Railgun were estimated to derive from Harmony, and funds were later deposited into three exchanges, with Binance and Huobi identifying, blocking, and seizing a portion (SRC-001). The evidence supports public attribution of both thefts to Lazarus Group and common laundering methods, but does not provide specific wallet addresses in the returned text and does not establish APT38 as a distinct supported label."}
